     bbssnnmmppggeett, bbssnnmmppwwaallkk and bbssnnmmppsseett are simple tools for retrieving manage‐
     ment information from and setting management information to a Simple Net‐
     work Management Protocol (SNMP) agent.

     Depending on the options bbssnnmmppggeett constructs either a SNMP GetRequest,
     GetNextRequest or a GetBulkRequest packet, fills in the object identi‐
     fiers (OIDs) of the objects whose values will be retrieved, waits for a
     response and prints it if received successfully.

     BBssnnmmppwwaallkk queries an agent with ether SNMP GetNextRequest or GetBulkRe‐
     quest packets, asking for values of OID instances that are a part of the
     object subtree rooted at the provided OIDs.

     BBssnnmmppsseett constructs a SNMP SetRequest packet, fills in the OIDs (object
     identifiers), syntaxes and values of the objects whose values are to be
     set and waits for a response from server.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     The options are as follows (not all apply to all three programs):

     --AA _o_p_t_i_o_n_s  Authentication options to use with SNMPv3 PDUs

                 pprroottoo==[[mmdd55||sshhaa]]
                   The protocol to use when calculating the PDU message di‐
                   gest.

                 kkeeyy==aauutthhkkeeyy
                   A binary localized authentication key to use when calculat‐
                   ing the PDU message digest.

                 By default SNMPv3 PDUs are sent unauthenticated.

     --aa          Skip any sanity checks when adding OIDs to a Protocol Data
                 Unit (PDU): ignore syntax/access type, allow adding of non-
                 leaf objects for GetPdu and read-only objects to a SetPDU.

     --bb _b_u_f_f_e_r_s_i_z_e
                 Tune the size of buffers used to send and receive packets.
                 The default size is 10000 bytes which should be enough unless
                 an agent sends a really large octetstring.  The maximum al‐
                 lowed length is 65535 according to the Structure of Manage‐
                 ment Information (SMIv2).

     --CC _o_p_t_i_o_n_s  The context to query with SNMPv3 PDUs.

                 ccoonntteexxtt==nnaammee
                   The context name.  Default is "" (empty).

                 ccoonntteexxtt--eennggiinnee==eennggiinnee--iidd
                   The SNMP Engine ID of the context to query with SNMPv3
                   PDUs, represented as binary octet string.  By default, this
                   is set to the Engine ID of the SNMP agent.

     --DD          Perform SNMP USM Engine Discovery, rather than sending a re‐
                 quest for the value of a specific object.

     --dd          Turn on debugging.  This option will cause the packets sent
                 and received to be dumped to the terminal.

     --ee          Retry on error.  If an error is returned in the response PDU,
                 resend the request removing the variable that caused the er‐
                 ror until a valid response is received.  This is only useful
                 for a GetRequest- and a GetNextRequest-PDU.

     --hh          Print a short help text with default values for various op‐
                 tions.

     --II _o_p_t_i_o_n_s  Load each MIB description file from the given list to trans‐
                 late symbolic object names to their numerical representation
                 and vice versa.  Use the other options to obtain a non-de‐
                 fault behaviour:

                 ccuutt==OOIIDD
                   Specifies the initial OID that was cut by gensnmpdef(1)
                   when producing the MIB description file.  The default value
                   is .iso(1).org(3).dod(6) which is what should have been
                   used for all the files installed under
                   _/_u_s_r_/_s_h_a_r_e_/_s_n_m_p_/_d_e_f_s.  Use this only if you generated your
                   own files, providing a --cc option to gensnmpdef(1).

                 ppaatthh==ffiilleeddiirr
                   The directory where files in the list will be searched.
                   The default is _/_u_s_r_/_s_h_a_r_e_/_s_n_m_p_/_d_e_f_s.

                 ffiillee==ffiilleelliisstt
                   A comma separated list of files to which the two options
                   above will apply.

                 The file suboption has to come after the other suboptions so
                 that their non-default values will be applied to the list of
                 files.  The order of the other suboptions before each file
                 suboption can be random.  Suboptions may be separated either
                 by commas or by spaces.  If using spaces make sure the entire
                 option string is one argument, for example using quotes.

     --ii _f_i_l_e_l_i_s_t
                 List of MIB description files produced by gensnmpdef(1) which
                 bbssnnmmppggeett, bbssnnmmppwwaallkk or bbssnnmmppsseett will search to translate nu‐
                 merical OIDs to their symbolic object names.  Multiple files
                 can be provided either giving this option multiple times or a
                 comma separated list of file names.  If a filename begins
                 with a letter the default directory, _/_u_s_r_/_s_h_a_r_e_/_s_n_m_p_/_d_e_f_s,
                 will be searched.

     --KK          Calculate and display the localized authentication and pri‐
                 vacy keys corresponding to a plain text password.  The pass‐
                 word is obtained via the environment.  Additionally, if one
                 or more OIDs are specified, the calculated keys are used when
                 processing the SNMPv3 requests.

     --ll _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
                 The path of the posix local (unix domain) socket if local
                 transport is used.

     --MM _m_a_x_-_r_e_p_e_t_i_t_i_o_n_s
                 The value for the max-repetitions field in a GetBulk PDU.
                 Default is 10.

     --NN _n_o_n_-_r_e_p_e_a_t_e_r_s
                 The value for the non-repeaters field in a GetBulk PDU.  De‐
                 fault is 0.

     --nn          Only use numerical representations for input and output OIDs
                 and do not try to resolve symbolic object names.  Note that
                 bbssnnmmppggeett, bbssnnmmppwwaallkk and bbssnnmmppsseett will print numerical OIDs
                 anyway if the corresponding string representation is not
                 found in the MIB description files.

     --oo _[_q_u_i_e_t_|_s_h_o_r_t_|_v_e_r_b_o_s_e_]
                 The format used to print the received response.  Quiet only
                 prints values, short (default) prints an abbreviated OID rep‐
                 resentation and the value.  In addition to the short output
                 verbose prints the type before the value.

     --PP _o_p_t_i_o_n_s  Privacy options to use with SNMPv3 PDUs

                 pprroottoo==[[aaeess||ddeess]]
                   The protocol to use when encrypting/decrypting SNMPv3 PDU
                   data.

                 kkeeyy==pprriivvkkeeyy
                   A binary localized privacy key to use when encrypting/de‐
                   crypting SNMPv3 PDU data.

                 By default plain text SNMPv3 PDUs are sent.

     --pp _[_g_e_t_|_g_e_t_n_e_x_t_|_g_e_t_b_u_l_k_]
                 The PDU type to send by bbssmmppggeett and bbssnnmmppwwaallkk.  Default is
                 get for bbssmmppggeett and getnext for bbssnnmmppwwaallkk.  Getbulk allows
                 executing the so called SNMP "bulkwalks" allowing the values
                 of multiple columns to be retrieved in a single PDU by
                 bbssnnmmppwwaallkk.

     --rr _r_e_t_r_i_e_s  Number of resends of request packets before giving up if the
                 agent does not respond after the first try.  Default is 3.

     --ss _[_t_r_a_n_s_:_:_]_[_c_o_m_m_u_n_i_t_y_@_]_[_s_e_r_v_e_r_]_[_:_p_o_r_t_]
                 Each of the server specification components is optional but
                 at least one has to be provided if the _s option is used.  The
                 server specification is constructed in the following manner:

                 ttrraannss::::
                   Transport type may be one of udp, stream or dgram.  If this
                   option is not provided an UDP inet/inet6 socket will be
                   used, which is the most common.  Stream stands for a posix
                   local stream socket and a posix local datagram socket will
                   be used if dgram is specified.

                 ccoommmmuunniittyy@@
                   Specify an SNMP community string to be used when sending
                   packets.  If the option is skipped the default "public"
                   will be used for bbssnnmmppggeett and bbssnnmmppwwaallkk and the default
                   "private" community string will be used for bbssnnmmppsseett.

                 sseerrvveerr
                   This might be either the IP address or the hostname where
                   the agent is listening.  The default is "localhost".

                 ppoorrtt
                   The destination port to send the requests to.  This is use‐
                   ful if the SNMP agent listens on a non-default port.  De‐
                   fault is given by the "snmp" entry in _/_e_t_c_/_s_e_r_v_i_c_e_s, port
                   161.

     --tt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t  Number of seconds before resending a request packet if the
                 agent does not respond.  The default value is 3 seconds.

     --UU _o_p_t_i_o_n_s  User credentials when sending SNMPv3 PDUs.

                 eennggiinnee==iidd
                   The Engine ID of the SNMP agent represented as a binary
                   octet string.

                 eennggiinnee--bboooottss==vvaalluuee
                   The value of the snmpEngineBoots of the SNMP agent.

                 eennggiinnee--ttiimmee==vvaalluuee
                   The value of the snmpEngineTime of the SNMP agent.

                   If any of the above is not specified, SNMP USM Engine Dis‐
                   covery is attempted.  This is also the default behavior.

                 nnaammee==uusseerrnnaammee
                   The USM user name to include in the SNMPv3 PDUs.  By de‐
                   fault, the user name is obtained via the environment.

     --vv _v_e_r_s_i_o_n  The SNMP protocol version to use when sending requests.  SNMP
                 versions 1, 2 and 3 are supported.  If no version option is
                 provided bbssnnmmppggeett, bbssnnmmppwwaallkk and bbssnnmmppsseett will use version 2.
                 Note that GetBulkRequest-PDUs were introduced in SNMPv2 thus
                 setting the version to 1 is incompatible with sending a Get‐
                 Bulk PDU.

     OID         The object identifier whose value to retrieve.  At least one
                 OID should be provided for bbssnnmmppggeett to be able to send a re‐
                 quest.

                 For bbssnnmmppwwaallkk this is the root object identifier of the sub‐
                 tree whose values are to be retrieved.  If no OID is provided
                 bbssnnmmppwwaallkk will walk the mib2 subtree rooted at
                 .iso(1).org(3).dod(6).internet(1).mgmt(2).mib2(1) .

                 Any of the formats used to print a single variable is valid
                 as input OID:

                 1.3.6.1.2.1.25.1.1.0

                 sysDescr

                 ifPhysAddress.1

                 ifRcvAddressStatus.2.6.255.255.255.255.255.255

                 ifRcvAddressType[2,ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]

                 ifRcvAddressStatus[Integer:1,OctetString:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
                   (requires the --oo _v_e_r_b_o_s_e option)

                 Square brackets are used to denote an entry's indexes.  When
                 used in an input OID, the square brackets may have to be es‐
                 caped or the OID has to be quoted to protect it from the
                 shell.  Note there is no difference between ifName.1 and "if‐
                 Name[1]".

     OID=_[_s_y_n_t_a_x:]_v_a_l_u_e
